            <ArticleTitle>Renal volumetry with magnetic resonance imaging.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>2058460117731120</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1177/2058460117731120</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">No gold standard exists for renal volumetry in vivo.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PURPOSE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To devise and evaluate segmentation methods on magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) datasets.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MATERIAL AND METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Five combinations of MRI pulse sequences and measuring methods were used to measure the renal volumes of five men aged 54-72 years scanned before autologous renal stem cell transplantation and three, six, and 12 months post transplantation.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Renal volume did not change after stem cell transplantation. The results varied considerably: the reproducibility (coefficient of variation) was 4.0-6.0% and measurements took 1-13 min per kidney. Manual segmentation of images from the volumetric interpolated breath-hold examination (VIBE) without fat saturation sequence provided best reproducibility but was time-consuming. Use of the ellipsoid formula from half Fourier acquisition single shot turbo spin echo (HASTE) provided the fastest measurement, but resulted in lower reproducibility.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Renal volumetry based on images from the pulse sequence VIBE without fat saturation acquired using an out-of-phase TE may be investigated further, possibly in combination with the quick ellipsoid formula.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
